From 554c6058b9e82a8e5a0cda713ac28750dfc4d105 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniil Gentili <daniil@daniil.it>
Date: Sat, 24 Oct 2026 17:55:08 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] eloop: Do not busy-loop with epoll when no timeout is
 registered

With CONFIG_ELOOP_EPOLL, eloop_run() initializes timeout_ms to -1 once
and then only updates it while a timeout is registered. Once the last
timeout fires, timeout_ms keeps its last value, usually 0 (the timeout
was due, or less than 1 ms away), and epoll_wait() returns immediately
on every iteration until a new timeout gets registered.

hostapd's RADIUS server hits this as soon as its last session expires:
it then burns a full CPU core while idle. Pass -1 when there is no
timeout, as the poll() path already does.

Signed-off-by: Daniil Gentili <daniil@daniil.it>
---
 src/utils/eloop.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

--- a/src/utils/eloop.c
+++ b/src/utils/eloop.c
@@ -1167,7 +1167,7 @@ void eloop_run(void)
 			res = 0;
 		} else {
 			res = epoll_wait(eloop.epollfd, eloop.epoll_events,
-					 eloop.count, timeout_ms);
+					 eloop.count, timeout ? timeout_ms : -1);
 		}
 #endif /* CONFIG_ELOOP_EPOLL */
 #ifdef CONFIG_ELOOP_KQUEUE
